 ],
 "literal": "Vast-place world's accumulated-yield great-wealth / all-together-with if-obtained, sugarcane's / stalk-cut piece's node-eaten-like / white-teeth-secreted fault-less sweet-water [of] / beauty-fashioned small-bracelets young-woman leaving-behind, / effort's side they-will-not-part, daily / relationship-manner custom's Death's / virtue-less crookedness having-known-they.",
 "translation": "Even if the great wealth of the vast world's accumulated yields were to be obtained all at once, those who truly know the virtue-less, crooked ways of Death, who comes daily in accordance with his custom, would never leave behind the young woman of beautiful, small bracelets, to go in search of wealth, parting from her sweet, faultless saliva that tastes like eating the sweet node of a cut piece of sugarcane.",
 "modern": "பரந்த இடத்தைக் கொண்ட உலகத்தின் திரண்ட பயனாகிய பெருஞ்செல்வம் முழுவதும் ஒருசேரக் கிடைப்பதாக இருந்தாலும், கரும்பின் தண்டை வெட்டிய துண்டின் கணுவைச் சுவைப்பது போன்ற, வெண்மையான பற்களிலிருந்து ஊறும் குற்றமற்ற இனிய நீரினையும், அழகிய சிறிய வளையல்களையும் உடைய இளமகளைப் பிரிந்து, முயற்சி மேற்கொண்டு செல்ல மாட்டார்கள்; நாள்தோறும் முறைப்படி உயிரைக் கவரும் கூற்றுவனின் அறமற்ற கோணல் வழியை அறிந்தவர்கள்.",
 "explanation": "The hero speaks to himself when contemplating leaving his beloved to earn wealth. He realizes that life is fleeting and Death is relentless and unjust, taking lives daily without any moral regard. Knowing this inevitable end, he decides that even the greatest wealth of the entire world is not worth parting from his beloved. Her sweet saliva is as delicious as the juice from a sugarcane node, and her presence is far more valuable than material gain. This poem belongs to the pālai tiṇai, where the theme of parting for wealth is contrasted with the stark reality of mortality.",
